    kwargs={"reasoning_content": "Now I understand. The reset() calls stop(False, ALL_PROFILE_ID). In stop(), if profile_state is in PARSE/BUILD, it calls wait_for_profile_state(profile_id, STATE_TX) — this hangs because the profile is stuck in PARSE/BUILD state (from killed runs, the profile may be stuck mid-build or the daemon has a stuck profile). \n\nActually wait — the previous timed-out runs: first two reached start() and ran. They were killed. When the client disconnects abruptly, the daemon may leave profiles in some state. On new connect, reset() finds a profile in PARSE/BUILD and waits for STATE_TX forever (never happens because no traffic is being driven).\n\nThe PARSE/BUILD → TX transition only happens if the profile is actually being built/started. A stale profile stuck in PARSE/BUILD hangs.\n\nSolution options:\n1. `c.reset(restart=True)` — same path, still calls stop.\n2. Clear profiles without going through the hanging stop. E.g., call lower-level: `clear_profile`, or restart daemon.\n3. Restart the TRex daemon service — heavy.\n\nActually, the simplest robust fix: avoid leaving stale profiles by always calling stop before disconnect. But I already have stale state now.\n\nLet me try to force-clear: The hang is in wait_for_profile_state during stop when state is PARSE/BUILD. If I can get the profile out... \n\nOption: use `c.clear_profile(block=False, pid_input=ALL_PROFILE_ID)`? That might send a command to clear. But if state is stuck PARSE/BUILD, clear might also wait.\n\nBetter: maybe the daemon isn't actually stuck; maybe there's genuinely no traffic but a profile lingering. Let me inspect current profile state via a quick script: connect, acquire(force=True), get_profile_list / states, without calling reset. Let me print profile states.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Perhaps the daemon connection from killed runs left a \"handler\" that holds the port. On re-acquire(force=True) it should steal. \n\nLet me write a diagnostic: connect, acquire(force=True), then call self.get_all_ports? and print profile states via low-level. Let me print `c.get_profile_list()` maybe exists. Let me inspect available methods and current states.\n\nAlternatively — the cleanest reliable approach: restart TRex daemon. But I don't know how to SSH. The daemon is on 10.99.80.222. I only have the Python client. There might be a way to restart via RPC? Not simple.\n\nLet me think about why wait_for_profile_state hangs. It loops calling _get_profile_state until it equals target or timeout? Looking at line 296 it's `time.sleep(0.001)` in a loop. Let me see the full function—it got truncated. Let me print just wait_for_profile_state source. It may have a timeout parameter. If it has no timeout, infinite loop.\n\nLet me inspect wait_for_profile_state fully, and also try get_profile_list / _get_profile_state.\n\nLet me write a diagnostic script.\n\nActually, maybe there IS a timeout in wait_for_profile_state but it's long. The truncated part. Let me re-run inspection for wait_for_profile_state and _get_profile_state and list methods.\n\nBut more productive: let me just try to clear stale state. Perhaps the issue is not really stale profile; maybe the very first run's traffic is STILL running on daemon (duration was 3s and 2s but those are TRex durations... actually duration param in start is seconds, mult=1, duration=3 → runs 3 sec then stops automatically!). \n\nWait — start(duration=3) means TRex auto-stops after 3 seconds! So after the first run's traffic auto-completed at 3s, the profile goes to IDLE or ASTF_LOADED state, NOT stuck PARSE/BUILD. Then when I killed the script (it was sleeping 4s then get_stats then stop), the traffic already ended. So the daemon left profile in ASTF_LOADED state (loaded but not transmitting). On next reset, stop() sees state ASTF_LOADED (not PARSE/BUILD, not TX) → skips stop → fine. Then check_states(ok=[ASTF_LOADED, IDLE]) ok.\n\nHmm, but it hung. So state must have been PARSE/BUILD. How? Maybe the first run's start() with duration=3 — but I killed at 30s? No, first run had time.sleep(4) then stats then stop, total should be ~ <10s but tool killed at 30s meaning it hung somewhere in first run too (get_stats or stop). Actually first run: start(duration=3,block=False), sleep(4), get_stats (this may hang?), stop. If get_stats hung... Actually the first version had duration=3. Elapsed 30s means it hung. Possibly get_stats() with sync hung, or prepare_port hung (resolve). We don't know.\n\nRegardless, now I'm stuck with possibly a PARSE/BUILD profile. Let me diagnose current daemon state without calling reset.\n\nLet me write diag script:\n```python\nc = make_astf_client()\nc.acquire(force=True)\n# list profiles\ntry:\n    pl = c.get_profile_list()\n    print(\"profiles:\", pl)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"get_profile_list err\", e)\n# print each state\nfor pid in (0,1,2):\n    try:\n        print(pid, c._get_profile_state(pid))\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(pid,\"err\",e)\n```\n\nLet me inspect for get_profile_list existence too. I'll just try/except.\n\nAlso want to try a hard cleanup: maybe call c.stop(block=False, pid_input=ALL_PROFILE_ID) to not block. Since block=False and not is_remove, if state is PARSE/BUILD it STILL calls wait_for_profile_state (blocking!) because that's before the block check. So block=False doesn't avoid the hang. \n\nThe hang line: `if profile_state in {PARSE, BUILD}: self.wait_for_profile_state(profile_id, STATE_TX)`. This is unconditional regardless of block. So any stop() while a profile is stuck in PARSE/BUILD hangs.\n\nSo I must get the profile OUT of PARSE/BUILD, or clear it via lower-level API that doesn't call stop's wait.\n\nOptions:\n- `c.clear_profile(block=False, pid_input=ALL_PROFILE_ID)` — let me check if clear_profile waits. Probably sends remove. Might work even in PARSE/BUILD? Uncertain.\n- Restart daemon.\n- Send raw RPC command.\n\nLet me first diagnose. Let me also print wait_for_profile_state full