 if res['err']: log(\"ERR\", label, res['err']); return False\n    log(\"OK\", label); return True\n```\n\nStages:\n- make_client (blocking connect; time-box 10)\n- acquire (time-box 10)\n- reset (time-box 8)\n- prepare_port (time-box 10)\n- load_profile (time-box 10)\n- start (time-box 10)\n- loop 3s stats\n- stop\n\nAnd overall alarm at 20... but with cumulative time-boxes we may exceed. Set alarm to 27 and rely on run harness 30s? Only 3s margin — risky. Let me lower each timeout and be selective.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the root cause instead of brute diagnosis. The hang at reset() — the most common TRex ASTF gotcha: after a client is killed while traffic running, the server is in a state where `reset()` waits to stop traffic. The recommended fix: call `c.stop(block=False)` or `c.stop()` before reset, or the server has an active profile.\n\nBut given the FIRST script (img_download_flood) hung at c.stop(block=True) — meaning traffic WAS running during that stop. So the server likely still thinks traffic is active. Even though ping is flat... maybe because the download recv(770000) never actually completes and no data flows? Or the start never actually sent because something.\n\nWait, actually in the first run, was traffic even sent? We never saw stats. The first run: make_client, acquire, reset, prepare_port, load_profile, start all presumably ok (else it'd hang earlier and we'd not know). Then loop, then stop(block=True) hung. If start succeeded with nc=True, traffic may be running. Killed at 30s. Server kept profile? Possibly.\n\nFor the probe runs, at reset it hangs — consistent with server having an un-stopped profile from run #1. So I need to STOP it first.\n\nSo: the fix is to call c.stop(block=False) BEFORE reset to clear the stuck profile. Let me do that. But will stop() also hang? stop with block=False should return quickly (just sends RPC). Let me try probe with order: acquire(force=True) → stop(block=False) → reset() → prepare_port → load_profile → start → loop → stop.\n\nAlso set duration for start to a positive short value so it auto-stops even if we fail to stop! start(duration=6000) sends for 6s then auto-stops on server. That prevents the \"stuck forever\" problem. Combined with nc=True. Good idea — use duration in ms so server self-stops. This is safer than duration=-1.\n\nLet me rewrite probe with:\n- start(mult=MULT, duration=6000, nc=True, block=False)  # ms? Actually ASTF start duration is in seconds? Let me check: start signature duration=-1. In ASTF, duration is in seconds (float). Hmm. The skill start signature: `start(self, mult=1, duration=-1, nc=False, block=True, latency_pps=0, ipv6=False, pid_input='_', client_mask=..., e_duration=0, t_duration=0, dump_interval=0)`. Duration unit: ASTF uses seconds. So duration=6 → 6 seconds. Let me set duration=5.\n\nWith duration=5 and block=False, server runs 5s then stops itself. Then sleep 6s, get_stats, then stop (should be no-op), disconnect.\n\nThis guarantees self-cleanup. \n\nAlso for reset hang: add stop(block=False) before reset.\n\nLet me also reduce alarm risk: set signal.alarm(26) and rely on self-stopping duration. Actually with self-stop duration=5, the whole script ~ make_client+acquire+stop+reset+prepare+load+start+6s = maybe 15-20s. Could approach 30. Let me trim sleep. Use duration=4, sleep loop 4.5s.\n\nLet me also keep the timed() thread approach? That adds complexity. Let me first just try adding stop before reset + self-stop duration, keep alarm 22. If reset still hangs, alarm will tell me STAGE=reset again.\n\nLet me write probe v2.\n\nI'll set:\n- signal.alarm(24)\n- order: make_client, acquire(force), stop(block=False) [clear stuck], reset, prepare_port, load_profile, start(duration=5, block=False), loop sleep 5, get_stats, stop(block=False), disconnect, os._exit.\n\nActually the disconnect at end with release_ports=False might hang? Put os._exit after. finally does os._exit.\n\nLet me write.", "refusal": null}
